import equational_theories.Definability.Regular
import equational_theories.Equations.All

/-!
# A 1024-operation clone

Source `883` is `x = y ◇ ((x ◇ y) ◇ (y ◇ y))` and two of its targets --
`870`, `x = y ◇ ((x ◇ x) ◇ (x ◇ y))`, and `917`, `x = y ◇ ((y ◇ y) ◇ (x ◇ y))` -- are settled by
`Magma.bigCert2` below: a model of `883` on `Fin 8` whose binary clone closes at `1024` operations,
none of which satisfies either.

This is a different magma from `Magma.negCert23` of `Definability.Clone870`, which also has a
`1024`-member clone on `Fin 8` but does not satisfy `883`; the two certificates share a shape and
nothing else.  No model of `883` in any of our pools with a smaller clone refutes anything.

At `1024` members the clone is far past what `![...]` can carry, so the tables are ASCII strings,
read back through `ByteArray`, which indexes in constant time.  `Magma.bigCert2Data` holds the clone
as `1024` blocks of `64` digits `0`-`7`, and `Magma.bigCert2Comp` holds each composite index
as two characters of base `40`, offset by `'0'` -- base `32` as in `Definability.Clone870`
would only reach `1024`.  The `1024 * 1024 * 64` equations of `Magma.IsCloneFamily.comp` are
checked by `native_decide`, so the refutations below additionally depend on `Lean.ofReduceBool`.
Each `native_decide` recompiles the module and the module is megabytes of string, so the calls are
batched into two: one conjunction for the clone family and one for the targets.

This file is generated by `genbig.py`.
-/

set_option maxHeartbeats 1000000
set_option maxRecDepth 4000

open Law Law.MagmaLaw

namespace Magma

/-- A model of equation `883` on `Fin 8` whose binary clone has `1024` members. -/
